WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.740
In the first film, a neighbour's pull turned one Notch receptor into one nuclear

2
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:14.020
signal. Now set two identical touching cells side by side: how do they pick opposite

3
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:19.680
fates? Nothing outside tells them which cell becomes nerve and which cell becomes

4
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.680
skin.

5
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.533
Both neighbours begin with the same toolkit.

6
00:00:24.793 --> 00:00:29.593
Each cell displays amber ligands to send and teal Notch receptors to receive.

7
00:00:30.873 --> 00:00:34.513
At the start, both pull equally on each other across their shared border.

8
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:39.293
Neither cell has a lead, and neither cell has committed to a fate.

9
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:43.827
Then random chance gives the left cell slightly more ligand.

10
00:00:44.947 --> 00:00:49.987
It pulls harder on the right cell's Notch receptors, releasing more cyan inner tail

11
00:00:49.987 --> 00:00:51.107
into the right nucleus.

12
00:00:52.487 --> 00:00:56.907
There, in the textbook model, Notch turns on repressors with two linked jobs.

13
00:00:58.147 --> 00:01:01.827
They block the nerve programme, and they lower the right cell's own sending.

14
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.530
Pause here and follow the loop one step back across the border.

15
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.150
The right cell now displays fewer amber ligands than before.

16
00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:16.770
What happens next to the Notch signal reaching the left cell, and to the left cell's

17
00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:17.770
own sending?

18
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:27.873
With less ligand pulling on its Notch, the left cell receives less inhibition.

19
00:01:29.293 --> 00:01:33.613
Its nerve programme stays active, and it puts even more ligand on its surface.

20
00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:39.293
The left cell lowers the right cell's sending, and the right cell's sending lowers

21
00:01:39.293 --> 00:01:44.853
the left's. Two brakes in a row make positive feedback: quieting its neighbour

22
00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:46.013
boosts the left cell.

23
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:51.533
When Notch lowers a cell's sending, equal neighbours split into opposite fates.

24
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:57.667
Watch the schematic curves below pull apart as each cycle widens that tiny head

25
00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:02.847
start. Soon the left cell sends without receiving and adopts the nerve fate.

26
00:02:04.107 --> 00:02:07.687
The right cell receives without sending and adopts the skin fate.

27
00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:12.207
Biologists call this neighbour-sorting circuit lateral inhibition.

28
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.080
Now zoom out from one pair to a whole sheet of touching cells.

29
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.680
Because the Notch signal neither diffuses nor multiplies, each cell compares itself

30
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.520
only with the neighbours it contacts.

31
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.700
Every emerging nerve precursor suppresses the ring of cells touching it, leaving

32
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.300
spaced nerve cells surrounded by skin.

33
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:40.013
Cells can also tune that spacing without abandoning direct contact.

34
00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:46.553
On a fly's back, cells send out thin moving extensions that touch neighbours a few

35
00:02:46.553 --> 00:02:52.833
cell widths away. Live imaging shows these contacts help space the bristles, still

36
00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:54.633
using touch rather than diffusion.

37
00:02:55.817 --> 00:03:01.197
In flies and worms alike, this mutual brake turns a small starting gap into a clean

38
00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:06.537
choice. Two neighbours need no outside map and no diffusing signal.

39
00:03:07.777 --> 00:03:12.637
By weakening a neighbour's ability to send back, a tiny difference grows into two

40
00:03:12.637 --> 00:03:13.477
opposite fates.
